  "description": "Compute forward-mode Jacobian-vector product and reverse-mode vector-Jacobian product for a 2-layer linear network.\n\nForward-mode AD (JVP) computes directional derivatives in O(N) operations when input dimension is small; reverse-mode AD (VJP) calculates loss gradients in O(1) passes for scalar objectives.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{JVP} = \\mathbf{W}_2 (\\mathbf{W}_1 \\mathbf{v}_{\\text{in}}), \\quad \\text{VJP} = (\\mathbf{v}_{\\text{out}}^T \\mathbf{W}_2) \\mathbf{W}_1\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute JVP directional pushforward by sequentially multiplying W1 * v_in then W2 * result\n2. Compute VJP adjoint pullback by multiplying v_out * W2 then result * W1\n3. Format output dictionary with 'jvp' and 'vjp' rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'jvp' and 'vjp' lists rounded to 4 decimal places.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: JVP and VJP for a 2-Layer Network\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nWhen training neural networks with gradient descent, our optimizers rely on derivatives to adjust weights in the direction that lowers the loss. If an analytical derivative formula has a bug, the model will learn slowly or fail to converge completely. **JVP and VJP for a 2-Layer Network** provides the mathematical principles to calculate, verify, or inspect these gradient signals.\n\nForward-mode AD (JVP) computes directional derivatives in O(N) operations when input dimension is small; reverse-mode AD (VJP) calculates loss gradients in O(1) passes for scalar objectives. In this problem, the goal is to: compute forward-mode jacobian-vector product and reverse-mode vector-jacobian product for a 2-layer linear network.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{JVP} = \\mathbf{W}_2 (\\mathbf{W}_1 \\mathbf{v}_{\\text{in}}), \\quad \\text{VJP} = (\\mathbf{v}_{\\text{out}}^T \\mathbf{W}_2) \\mathbf{W}_1\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute JVP directional pushforward by sequentially multiplying W1 * v_in then W2 * result\n2. **Phase 2**: Compute VJP adjoint pullback by multiplying v_out * W2 then result * W1\n3. **Phase 3**: Format output dictionary with 'jvp' and 'vjp' rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Autograd Validation**: In machine learning frameworks like PyTorch and JAX, gradient verification tests custom backward functions against numerical approximations.\n* **Numerical Precision**: Choosing the right step size or threshold is critical. A perturbation that is too large causes truncation error, while one that is too small leads to floating-point roundoff error.\n* **Computational Cost**: While numerical approximations are useful for checking correctness, analytical gradients computed via backpropagation are far more efficient for training because they evaluate in a single backward pass.",
