  "starterCode": "import numpy as np\n\ndef calculus_jvp_vjp(x: list, W1: list, W2: list, v_in: list, v_out: list) -> dict:\n    pass",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **JVP and VJP for a 2-Layer Network** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef calculus_jvp_vjp(x: list, W1: list, W2: list, v_in: list, v_out: list) -> dict:\n    w1, w2 = np.array(W1, dtype=float), np.array(W2, dtype=float)\n    return {'jvp': np.round(w2 @ (w1 @ np.array(v_in, dtype=float)), 4).tolist(), 'vjp': np.round((np.array(v_out, dtype=float) @ w2) @ w1, 4).tolist()}\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
